CHAP GLOSSARY

Canonical terms of the Universal Drift Framework

Drift
The continuous deformation of a system across time; the structural impossibility of perfect sameness.

Operator
A directional influence that pushes a system away from perfect identity (structural, behavioural, constraint, interaction, emergent, noise).

Total Drift
The composite field formed by all drift operators acting simultaneously.

State Manifold
The structural space of all configurations a system can occupy.

Drift Basin
A region of the manifold where drift slows and stabilisation is strong.

Escape
The transition out of a basin when accumulated drift exceeds resistance.

Curvature
The bending of a system’s trajectory through its manifold; the geometric signature of instability.

Torsion
The twisting of a system’s trajectory; the geometric signature of deep nonlinear behaviour.

Surface
A structural corridor that channels long‑term drift behaviour.

Velocity
The rate at which a system moves through its manifold.

Acceleration
The change in drift velocity over time.

Jerk
The change in acceleration; the dynamic precursor to cascades and instability.

Pressure
External forces acting on drift (environmental, relational, contextual).

Resistance
Internal forces that slow drift (stabilisers, constraints, inertia).

Energy
The system’s capacity for structural or behavioural change.

Stabilisation
Mechanisms that slow, shape, or absorb drift without eliminating it.

Managed Drift
Drift that remains within tolerable bounds due to stabilisation.

Continuality
The illusion of sameness created when drift moves slowly enough to be absorbed into the present.

Drift Cycle
The universal rhythm of change: drift → interaction → emergence → cascade.

Pattern Evolution
The long‑term reshaping of a system’s internal logic as drift accumulates.

